    "explanation": "Rider and driver: I have decided this case on the platform's own records, and no money moves.\n\nRider — why the charge stands. Your fare is metered, and the distance is checked against the GPS trace, which governs over the rounded figure shown on the trip record. The trace measures the route actually driven at 21.79 km against an optimal route of 20.4 km: an excess of 1.39 km, or 6.81%. The route-deviation rule requires the excess to be more than 20% of the optimal distance AND more than 0.8 km, and both limbs must pass. The 0.8 km limb is met, but the percentage limb is not — 6.81% is far short of 20% — so no deviation is established, and the policy directs no action in that situation however the trip felt. I understand it took 32 minutes against a 27-minute estimate and that it was not the route you would have chosen, but this gate is a distance gate and it is not crossed. Under no action the SGD 29.91 charged stands exactly as it is; nothing is returned to you.\n\nDriver — your explanation was not needed, and nothing was found against you. Your in-app message about the BKE crawl is on the record, and there is no LTA traffic advisory for that corridor in the bundle (the advisory artifact exists only as a missing record, weight 0.0, issue MISSING_ARTIFACT). The road-closure / traffic-advisory exoneration therefore could not have been run. It does not matter: because no deviation was established on the distance test, no exoneration was required, and no charge is affected either way.\n\nOn the request to escalate. The rider asks for a human review on the basis that the traffic excuse is unevidenced. I have not escalated. Escalation is reserved for cases the record cannot decide; this record decides the case on the threshold test, and the missing advisory would only have mattered if a deviation had been established and the driver had needed to excuse it. No exculpatory clause was invoked by the driver — his case, correctly, is that the threshold is not met — and a general assertion that traffic was bad is not a clause invocation. The genuinely open question, whether there really was a BKE slowdown around 11:29 on 19 July, is noted here but does not change the money, because none is due.\n\nChecks I ran. The trace is unimpaired: 193 fixes at 10-second intervals, 100% coverage, no gaps, so no trace-integrity issue arises. The fare breakdown carries no ERP gantries, no tolls and no airport surcharge, and every line reconciles to the SGD 29.91 charged, so there is no billing-integrity correction under POL-RD-09(4). The distance billed (21.8 km) is not less than the distance traced (21.79 km), so no driver compensation arises under POL-RD-04. No clause in this policy produces a referral on these facts.\n\nConfidence 0.9: the deciding finding rests on the GPS trace and the trip record, both at full weight (1.0)."
